# Local analysis venv. System python is externally managed, so analyze.py
# needs this to draw figures - but it is 181 MB of rebuildable wheels.
#   python3 -m venv .venv && .venv/bin/pip install matplotlib
.venv/
__pycache__/

# Perfetto traces: ~20 MB each, 1.2 GB across both matrices. They are
# diagnostic (sched_switch evidence for cluster placement), not measurement -
# nothing in analyze.py reads them, and every number in results*.json is
# derived from bench_json/ and telemetry/ instead. Too big for git; keep them
# locally or archive them out of band.
out/*/traces/

# Runtime noise
out/*/receiver.log
out/*/receiver.pid
out/.prep/
# The receiver rewrites this every ~30 s while it is up, so tracking it means
# the tree is permanently dirty and every run produces a spurious diff. It is
# liveness state, not measurement - the one thing under inbox/ worth ignoring.
out/*/inbox/.heartbeat

# NOTE what is deliberately NOT ignored:
#   out/*/inbox/        the received measurement arms, ~2.6 MB total. ingest.py
#                       is a pure function of this directory, so keeping it
#                       means results can be rebuilt after any ingest fix -
#                       which is exactly what recovered a wrongly-rejected arm
#                       and a trace-clobbering bug during the first two runs.
#   out/*/bench_json/   untouched llama-bench output; the source of truth.
#   out/*/telemetry/    per-arm sampler CSVs; energy is re-derivable from them.

# Release artifacts: built by hand, uploaded to GitHub Releases, not committed.
dist/

# editor swap files - a stray .swp nearly landed in a submission commit
*.swp
*.swo
*~
